ter and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}) & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}) & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(0,\\frac{1}{4})$ and its radius is $\\frac{3}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-103896/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98e3f3b4-8dfa-515c-90b1-09fb19d68144"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-103896"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
